    {
      "id": "cmtg6w8y1002c04i8xk1wrtsr",
      "term": "How do you determine whether or not a deal destroys/creates value? How can it look/be accretive but destroy value?",
      "setTitle": "M&A ",
      "klps": [
        {
          "text": "The premium paid is a prepayment of expected synergies — the deal creates value only if the present value of synergies exceeds that premium",
          "kind": "condition"
        },
        {
          "text": "If the premium exceeds the synergies, the combined entity is worth less than what was paid for it — that is value destruction",
          "kind": "definition"
        },
        {
          "text": "Accretion is an accounting test — whether combined EPS rises — and is separate from the economic question of whether value was created",
          "kind": "contrast"
        },
        {
          "text": "The premium can exceed the present value of realized synergies after the deal closes, destroying value even though the deal was underwritten as accretive",
          "kind": "mechanism"
        },
        {
          "text": "Buying a lower-P/E target with stock mechanically raises EPS even when no real value is created, so accretion alone can signal value creation when there is none",
          "kind": "mechanism"
        },
        {
          "text": "If the target's value declines after the deal, its P/E stays below the buyer's so EPS still looks accretive, yet combined value is less than the two standalone companies",
          "kind": "causal"
        },
        {
          "text": "The deal itself can cause deterioration — consolidated revenue can fall below the two companies' separate revenues through customer loss or channel conflict",
          "kind": "example"
        },
        {
          "text": "Hidden liabilities discovered post-close erode the value the premium was underwritten by, another way an accretive deal destroys value",
          "kind": "condition"
        }
      ],
