      "klps": [
        {
          "text": "A fairness opinion is a letter from an independent financial advisor to a company's board on whether the transaction is fair from a financial point of view.",
          "kind": "definition"
        },
        {
          "text": "The board uses the fairness opinion to support its recommendation to shareholders and to satisfy its fiduciary duty not to mislead them.",
          "kind": "mechanism"
        },
        {
          "text": "It provides the board meaningful protection against shareholder litigation over the price.",
          "kind": "causal"
        },
        {
          "text": "The word independent matters: the advisor must be free of conflicts so the valuation of the target is genuinely unbiased.",
          "kind": "condition"
        },
        {
          "text": "A fairness opinion contains a range of valuations of the target based on the analyses performed, typically DCF, trading comparables, and precedent transactions, and presents the valuation as a range rather than a single number.",
          "kind": "example"
        },
        {
          "text": "A fairness opinion contains the assumptions, limitations, and qualifications of the analyses — what the advisor relied on, what was excluded, and where the analysis could be wrong.",
          "kind": "definition"
        },
        {
          "text": "A fairness opinion ends with the advisor's explicit conclusion on whether the price or consideration is fair from a financial point of view.",
          "kind": "definition"
        }
      ],
